cartridge "BATTER UP!/J.C. HILTY/1987"

' BATTER UP!
' A ColecoVision game based on "Batter Up!" by J.C. Hilty, Ahoy! August
' 1987 (Commodore 64, BASIC). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game (the original's rules, read from its listing, orig/batter.bas):
' nine innings of baseball; you bat, the computer pitches. Each of your
' nine batters has a batting average, drawn at random (100 to 399) for the
' game. Each pitch is one of eight, half of them strikes (over the plate)
' and half balls (high, outside, low and inside, low). A swing at a strike
' meets the ball; a swing at a ball is a strike; no swing is a called
' strike or a ball. Where a hit goes is a random number plus the batter's
' average: HOME RUN, TRIPLE, DOUBLE, SINGLE, FOUL BALL (a strike unless
' there are two), GROUND OUT, FLY OUT, in the listing's proportions
' (E = RND + AVERAGE/1000 against 1.1, .9, .8, .6, .5, .3). Three strikes
' are an out, four balls a walk, three outs end your half inning. Runners
' move up as many bases as the hit (a home run with the bases loaded is a
' GRAND SLAM). The computer's half inning is a random 0 to 3 runs. After
' nine innings the scoreboard shows who won.
'
' Improvements and liberties:
'   - Timing matters: the ball flies from the pitcher's hand to the plate
'     at one of three speeds (42, 34 or 27 frames). A swing within 5 frames
'     of the ball reaching the plate meets a strike, but each frame off the
'     perfect moment takes .080 off the hit roll (a perfect swing gets the
'     listing's odds); 6 to 8 frames off is a foul tip (a foul ball);
'     further off is a swing and a miss. On the C64 any press while the
'     ball moved met a strike with the full odds, which scored about nine
'     runs an inning against the computer's 1.5. Swinging at the same
'     moment every pitch, or never swinging, does not win (see README.md).
'   - A walk forces runners up only as needed (the listing moved every
'     runner a base, as for a single). The listing's extra lineup step
'     after the computer's half inning (it skipped a batter) is dropped.
'   - Two players: controller 1 bats for the GREENS, controller 2 for the
'     BLUES, each pitch thrown by the computer; the BLUES bat in the bottom
'     of each inning and skip the bottom of the ninth when ahead. One
'     player: the GREENS against the computer's BLUES, as in the listing.
'   - No team name entry (no keyboard): the GREENS and the BLUES. The
'     GREENS' lineup is the listing's (ROCKY ... HAWK); the BLUES' is new.
'     The lineup is shown on screen instead of printed.
'   - A ball park seen from behind home plate, a batter with three swing
'     poses, the pitcher's wind-up, the ball growing as it comes in, the hit
'     ball flying off, runners on the bases, a catcher; a title tune, sound
'     effects; the game ends on the scoreboard, then the title.
'
' Controls: a side button swings (controller 1 in a one-player game, either
' works; in two players, the batting team's controller). On the title, up
' and down choose, a button starts. No keypad.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers. Nothing flashes.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs cliff-div-left-in-sum, batter-shift-by-expr, jake-return-before-for removed 2026-11-01.

